% composition-series: Ontario Listening Bridges — set 1
% composition-grade: 5
% composition-elements: offbeat entries after eighth rests; dotted quarter followed by an eighth (tam-ti); staccato articulation; I-V harmony (C and G); verse-chorus contrast, with call and response between two groups in the chorus
% composition-listen: The Skatalites — "Guns of Navarone" (Dimitri Tiomkin's 1961 film theme arranged as ska; produced by Coxsone Dodd, issued in Jamaica in 1965 and in the UK on Island the same year, B-side "Marcus Garvey"; UK chart entry 1967)
% composition-listen-url: https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=The+Skatalites+Guns+of+Navarone+1965
% composition-bridge: Hear the offbeat accompaniment, horn conversation and buoyant ensemble precision.
% composition-context: The Skatalites are foundational to Jamaican ska and its links with mento, jazz, R&B and later reggae. This original classroom song uses offbeat rests as a comparison, not a ska claim.
% composition-distance: Original melody, lyric and I-V harmony; no horn riff, bass line, film-theme melody or recording arrangement is copied.
% improv: {"id":"A","bars":"3-4","role":"horn or recorder pair","prompt":"Answer in the rest after 'lead'","choices":["copy 'drummer lead'","two offbeat notes","stay silent"],"replyBars":1}
% improv: {"id":"B","bars":"9-12","role":"group 2","prompt":"How does group 2 answer?","choices":["sing it","play it on recorder","clap its rhythm"],"replyBars":1}
% dossier-function: original sung listening-bridge song for Grade 5; a verse that enters off the beat, then a call-and-response chorus between two groups that lands on the beat, with an instrumental reply in the rests of bars 4 and 8
% dossier-elements: sixteen bars in 4/4; C major, pitch set C-D-E-F-G-A-B (F only in bar 7), range C4-E5; verse (bars 1-8): bars 1, 2, 5 and 6 begin each beat with an eighth rest and enter on the offbeat with staccato notes, bars 3 and 7 answer on the beat with a dotted quarter and eighth (tam-ti), bars 4 and 8 hold a half note then rest two beats; chorus (bars 9-16): bars 9-12 are four one-bar calls and answers in the rhythm dotted quarter, three eighths, quarter, group 1 sings bars 9 and 11 and group 2 answers in bars 10 and 12 (the groups are named here, not in the score); bars 13-16 all together, staccato quarters on the beat in bar 13, a half note in bar 14, tam-ti in bar 15, ending on C5; C-G (I-V) only; verse-chorus form, 8+8 bars
% dossier-practice: Quarter note = 120 is the target; begin at 92 and speak the verse over a steady clap on the beats, singing only the words that fall between the claps. Rehearsal key C major; written range C4-E5, inside the Grade 4-8 voice range; against the young-unison C4-D5 range only one E5 (bar 11) lies above, so transpose down a tone if needed. Teach in steps: clap beats while saying the offbeat words; sing the verse; learn both halves of the chorus as one group; split into groups 1 and 2; add a horn or recorder pair answering in the rests of bars 4 and 8.
% dossier-accompaniment: Newly authored C-G (I-V) chords played by the page's pop band, chosen as a neutral, straight-eighth groove; it is not a ska rhythm section and must not be labelled ska, mento or reggae. Classroom option: one group plays short chords on the offbeats (guitar, ukulele or keyboard) so students can compare their own offbeat comping with the recording's, without copying its parts.
% dossier-lyrics: Original English words written for this record; every note has one syllable, and the offbeat words are short so they can be sung crisply. Sung piece: verse in unison, chorus in two groups then all together. No words come from the reference, which is instrumental, or from the film song.
% dossier-honesty: AI-assisted original composition for Grand Central Stationery, 11 November 2026, under human-directed range, rhythm and teaching constraints. Not traditional, not collected from any community and not modelled on the reference's melody, horn lines or arrangement; the Skatalites recording is a listening bridge only.
% dossier-licence: CC0 1.0 for the melody, words, chords and this ABC encoding. "Guns of Navarone" (Dimitri Tiomkin and Paul Webster) and the Skatalites' recording remain their owners' copyright and are not included.
% dossier-respect: Name the Skatalites as Kingston session musicians (among them Tommy McCook, Roland Alphonso, Don Drummond, Lloyd Knibb and Jackie Mittoo) who shaped ska in the years around Jamaican independence in 1962; explain that "Guns of Navarone" is their arrangement of a Hollywood film theme. Teach Jamaican authorship and chronology, and do not collapse ska, rocksteady, reggae and generic "island music"; never present this classroom song as ska.
% dossier-review: Grade 5 classroom music teacher, Jamaican music educator, second score reader and student singing trial; all reviews still required
