N:HOW READ (2026-11-11): No source plate exists because this is a new composition. Bar-by-bar audit, every bar eight eighths: bars 1, 2, 5 and 6 alternate eighth rests with staccato offbeat notes for three beats and then sing beat 4 (E G A G E; D G A G; B C B D C; C A G E); bars 3 and 7 are quarter, quarter, dotted quarter, eighth (E G A G; D G F D); bars 4 and 8 are a half note (D4, then C4) and a half rest. Chorus bars 9-12 are dotted quarter, eighth, eighth, eighth, quarter (C G A C G; B G A B G; C G A C E; D B A B G); bar 13 four staccato quarters C5 G E G; bar 14 B A quarters and G half; bar 15 dotted quarter, eighth, quarter, quarter (D5 C B G); bar 16 C5 half and half rest. Pitch set C-D-E-F-G-A-B, range C4-E5, largest leap inside a phrase a fifth (G4-D5 into bar 15), with fourths C5-G4 in the chorus; the one octave, C4 up to C5, crosses the half rest from the verse into the chorus (bars 8-9). Form: verse-chorus 8+8. Originality check: the offbeat E-G-A-G figure and the "Who can find the space?" call were compared with "Guns of Navarone" (the film theme and the Skatalites' horn line) and with common children's and pop songs; no shared hook was found; offbeat entries and a do-so-la cell are common property.
N:UNCERTAIN: Whether Grade 5 singers place the offbeat entries cleanly at quarter = 120, whether the chorus groups hand over without a gap, and how the pop band sits under the offbeat verse can only be settled by a classroom trial. The originality check is one reader's; a second score reader should repeat it. The Jamaican release details (label credit, catalogue numbers and the 1967 chart position) come from discographies and must be checked against an authorized source.
